            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Laser cutting sheet metal means a focused fibre laser beam melts the metal along a programmed path while a jet of gas blows it out of the cut. A CNC cutter does this straight from a file: a narrow kerf, a clean edge and identical parts every time, with no cutting disc or template. In a small shop, the xTool MetalFab can take on this job – a handheld 800 W or 1200 W laser welder that becomes a fibre laser cutter for metal with its CNC module.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How does laser cutting sheet metal work?' ],
                [ 'p', 'A fibre laser produces an infrared beam that an optical fibre carries to the cutting head. A lens focuses it into a spot a fraction of a millimetre across, where the power density is high enough to melt the metal through the full thickness of the sheet. The head follows the path from your file, so holes and corners come out the same on the first part and the hundredth. The kerf is narrow and the heat acts on a small area, so the sheet distorts less than with plasma or an oxy-fuel torch.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'What does the assist gas do?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Gas flows through the nozzle together with the beam. It blows molten metal out of the kerf, protects the optics and determines what the edge looks like:' ],
